 of five point nine percent  in the economy in Europe where they use  the euro so excluding the UK five point  seven percent in Japan don't buy five  2% and China growth of just 1% now it  looks as if China is getting out of this  quite well but when you consider that  last year they reported growth of 6% you  can see just how severely that has hit  their economy what the IMF says is that  when all this is done you have to get  away from the idea of D globalization  because that wouldn't help the global  economy and also there has to be fiscal  stimulus across several countries it has  to be coordinated it can't just be  piecemeal and they say in that way that  could rebuild the global economy by 2021  but as I say a stark assessment from the  International Monetary Fund and as far  as the US economy is concerned  specifically Alan where you are Donald  Trump is saying look I am the guy who  can or will choose not to restart the  economy when the time is right other  people saying no it has to happen in  stages and on top of that what you had  what claims for 16 17 million people  putting in claims for Social Security in  the states in the space of 3 weeks and  that's never happened before  ever in the US no Donald Trump to be  clear Donald Trump doesn't have the  power to order States to reopen what he  can do is put a great deal of pressure  on a number of states a number of  Governors and say look you have to do  this but he doesn't have the power to  reopen the economy and many states are  very much in line with public thinking  in this which is we will reopen when the  scientists see it see though there are a  number of organizations who are tied to  donald trump's reelection effort who  would be regarded in this country it's  right-wing who are saying that they are  going to start organizing protests  demanding that the economy be reopened  and it may well be there is a gradual  reopening but you remember that donald  trump was talking about having the  economy up and running again by Easter  well that was last Sunday he's no  talking about me the first that seems  unlikely when a number of states are  saying that their peak is still two  weeks away so that would take us towards  that date this is going to be a long  slow process and you have to see it  through Donald Trump's eyes he sees it  through the prism of  if there is a strong economy then he  will be real  that's what he believes that is what his  campaign has been building on and that  is what been building up to the point  when the virus hit and he thinks if this  continues then the economy will be  wrecked he will get the blame and  therefore he won't be reelected that is  why he is very keen to reopen the  economy as soon as possible  but looking at the figures from the IMF  and what Donald Trump is saying it seems  unlikely that in the second quarter  which would take us through to June for  those who don't speak financial talk  which would take us to the end of June  they are saying that this is going to  impact the global economy and it's going  to impact the United States and it could  well go on for a period after that as  well Ellen thanks very much let's bring  in Gareth lever he's a senior economist  at Capital Economics a consultancy in  London Gareth welcome back to al-jazeera  these numbers are staggering what do  they mean and they are and I think that  is because the economic shock that we're  facing now is unprecedented the large  parts of the global economy are  effectively shut down to contain the  spread of the coronavirus and until  health officials and governments are  confident that the virus has been  contained these measures will remain in  place and the economic shock will  continue we're seeing some economies  begin just to loosen the strictures  inside which they've been operating lock  downs or partial lock downs or near lock  downs will the easing of those economic  stays if you will go any way to mitigate  the staggering figures t