eap  loans to companies to make sure that  they can they can get through the crisis  but all this is really kind of disaster  relief at the moment is to make sure  that these companies can get through the  crisis I think in the third quarter  you'll need another boost from  governments as well to provide the extra  support to demand that's going to be  lacking from the private sector because  of unemployment and higher debt levels  and so on careth leather thank you very  much  thank you well as if that economic news  wasn't bad enough the world's airlines  now stand to lose more than three  hundred billion dollars in revenue this  year that's the latest forecast from  IATA that's the overall industry's  representative body carriers have  grounded aircraft and crew of course  amid a growing number of travel  restrictions and border closures let's  go straight to our economics editor a  Batali who joins us on skype from London  Abbott great to be here on the NewsHour  this is kind of the blow that the  airline industry was waiting on landing  on top of it wasn't it Peter you're  absolutely right you know the industry  employs  some in the region are 65 million people  directly and indirectly and so you know  they're expecting 25 million jobs could  be lost over the next three months  unless governments step in with health  now we had in the United States that the  Trump administration that has said  they'll give sixty billion dollars to  the airline industry but the airline  industry has been reluctant to take  those on because part of that money  comes in the form of loans and quite  rightly so many people would think and  some of the money cannot be used to buy  back shares or dividends or any or pay  executives huge bonuses as has been the  case in the previous previous financial  crisis in 2008 so they're reluctant to  take the money and when you look at the  for instance in the UK the British  government has said listen you know  we're not going to hand out huge  bailouts to help put the airlines and  when we'll take each airline case by  case and see see what support we can  offer but the first port of call you  need to go to your shareholders to get  your money and you got really share  waste now then in the process of for  loaning 30,000 employees so this is very  real economy kind of stuff this is  people's lives being affected and they  put this into the wider context because  the global tourism industry is such a  huge industry it's about almost nine  trillion dollars is the actual value of  it and it employs more than 300 people  around the world and it's not just  Airlines it's hotels it's cruise ships  and it's it's your air B &amp; Bs you know  little the little guys are suffering as  much as the big guys okay when we talk  about the little guys and the big big  guys in the context of the airline  industry I noticed on Twitter British  Airways is scrapping one 747 per day  every day this week  so my question is this will this  directly impact the low-cost carriers  for whom the profit margins are very  small or the big legacy carriers the  share ways the American Airlines the the  Qantas type airline set up you know  those big legacy carriers who've been  around for 50 60 70 years so very  interesting question now here's the  thing British Airways has taken and  pontius they've taken lessons from the  low-cost airlines and so they've been  reducing their costs you know when you  go internet onto an aeroplane you know  they'll be giving you a sandwich rather  than a full meal and they'll be reducing  other other costs as well along the way  so you know you're paying for your  baggage just like you do for the  low-cost airlines this is gonna hit all  airlines I don't think that you know is  this this pandemic pandemic or  discriminate as to you know who gets  affected the most  so all airlines will need some kind of  funding or will need some kind of boring  to get them through this particular  process in the US for instance if if  small airlines are worrying they're  under 100 million dollars they don't  have to abide by the rules which say you  know you can't use this money for  anything anything else like dividends or  whatever this this is money for you to  keep people in jobs and so I think  you're going to see the same kind of  support around the world from from from  government to to Airlines which will be  specific to say this money that we give  you is entirely for keeping people in  work and anything else you need to go to  your shareholders to find the money  habit thanks very much

